Kubernetes isn’t just about pods and clusters. It’s built on objects — each with a job to do.

Here’s what they are, and why they matter:

1. Pod – The smallest unit. It runs your app containers.
2. Deployment – Keeps your pods running and replaces them if they fail.
3. ReplicaSet – Makes sure the right number of pods are always running.
4. StatefulSet – Like deployments, but for apps that need stable storage or identity.
5. Job – Runs a task once and stops. Great for batch work.
6. DaemonSet – Runs one pod on every node. Useful for logging or monitoring agents.
7. Service – Gives your pods a stable way to talk to each other.
8. Ingress – Controls external access to your services (like a smart gate).
9. NetworkPolicy – Sets rules for who can talk to whom inside the cluster.
10. Volume – Temporary storage for pods.
11. PersistentVolume – Long-term storage that survives pod restarts.
12. Secret – Stores sensitive info like passwords or tokens.
13. Namespace – Splits your cluster into isolated environments.
14. ServiceAccount – Gives pods an identity to access the cluster securely.
15. Roles – Controls what users or pods can do in the cluster.

Learn these. They’re the building blocks of every Kubernetes setup.

